🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗣𝗼𝗹𝗶𝗰𝘆 — 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗖𝗮𝗻'𝘁 𝗬𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗺𝘂𝗻𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗲?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your application Pods are Running, but one Pod cannot communicate with another Pod in the same Kubernetes cluster. How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately restart the Pods. First, I'd check whether the issue is caused by network policies, DNS, application ports, or the cluster network.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Identify the affected Pods

kubectl get pods -A -o wide

Check Pod IPs, namespaces, and whether the issue affects specific workloads.

2️⃣ Test connectivity

From the source Pod, test the destination Pod or Service on the required application port.

kubectl exec -it <source-pod> -n <namespace> -- sh

Use available tools such as "curl" or "nc" to test connectivity.

3️⃣ Check DNS resolution

nslookup <service-name>

Confirm whether the destination Service name resolves correctly.

4️⃣ Inspect NetworkPolicies

kubectl get networkpolicy -A
kubectl describe networkpolicy <policy-name> -n <namespace>

Check ingress and egress rules, Pod selectors, namespace selectors, and allowed ports.

5️⃣ Verify Service configuration

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

Confirm the Service selector matches the intended Pods and that endpoints are available.

6️⃣ Investigate cluster networking

If the configuration looks correct, check CNI plugin health, node connectivity, routing, firewall rules, and relevant network logs.

7️⃣ Verify the fix

Retest communication from the affected source Pod and confirm the application works as expected.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Running Pods do not guarantee network connectivity.

Follow the traffic path:

Source Pod → DNS → NetworkPolicy → Cluster Network → Destination Pod

Identify the failing layer before changing production configuration.
